  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(3)=-3$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=3$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=-3+0=-3.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=-3.$",
